{"sounds":{"intro":"Lithuanian is a Baltic language, one of the oldest living Indo-European languages, closely related to Latvian. It uses the Latin alphabet with extra marks. Vowels with a hook (ą, ę, į, ų) or a bar (ū) are long. The dot over ė also makes a long, closed \"e\". Stress can fall on any syllable and moves between forms of the same word.","rows":[["ą ę į ų","long vowels, \"ah\", \"eh\", \"ee\", \"oo\"","ąžuolas"],["ė","long, closed \"e\", like the \"ay\" in \"say\" without the glide","ėjau"],["y","long \"ee\" (the same as į)","mylėti"],["č","\"ch\" as in \"church\"","čia"],["š","\"sh\" as in \"ship\"","šuo"],["ž","\"s\" as in \"measure\"","žuvis"],["ie / uo","a glide: \"ie\" like \"ye\" in \"yet\" and \"uo\" like \"wo\" in \"word\"","kiek, šuo"]],"notes":["Lithuanian has seven cases and no articles. The words change their endings to show their job in the sentence.","Marks on vowels (accents) show stress in dictionaries but are not written in normal text. The \"say\" line shows the usual stress. The pool is unreviewed and a few rare words are best guesses."]},"numbers":[{"id":"0-to-10","title":"0 to 10","items":[["zero","nulis","NOO-lis"],["one","vienas","VEE-eh-nas"],["two","du","doo"],["three","trys","trees"],["four","keturi","keh-too-REE"],["five","penki","pen-KEE"],["six","šeši","sheh-SHEE"],["seven","septyni","sep-tee-NEE"],["eight","aštuoni","ash-too-OH-nee"],["nine","devyni","deh-vee-NEE"],["ten","dešimt","deh-SHIMT"]]},{"id":"11-to-20","title":"11 to 20","items":[["eleven","vienuolika","vee-eh-NOO-oh-lee-kah"],["twelve","dvylika","DVEE-lee-kah"],["thirteen","trylika","TREE-lee-kah"],["fourteen","keturiolika","keh-too-ree-OH-lee-kah"],["fifteen","penkiolika","pen-kee-OH-lee-kah"],["sixteen","šešiolika","sheh-shee-OH-lee-kah"],["seventeen","septyniolika","sep-tee-nee-OH-lee-kah"],["eighteen","aštuoniolika","ash-too-oh-nee-OH-lee-kah"],["nineteen","devyniolika","deh-vee-nee-OH-lee-kah"],["twenty","dvidešimt","dvee-DEH-shimt"]]},{"id":"tens-and-big-n